WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.500
In Florida, the sun injures more charter customers than anything else, so learn to tell these apart.

2
00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:11.575
Heat exhaustion: pale, cool, sweaty skin, with weakness and nausea.

3
00:00:11.825 --> 00:00:15.950
Get them into shade, cool them, give fluids, and let them rest.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.700
Heat stroke is a different animal.

5
00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:21.675
Hot skin, confusion, or collapse.

6
00:00:21.925 --> 00:00:24.250
So what’s the difference you look for?

7
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.125
The skin and the mind.

8
00:00:27.375 --> 00:00:29.700
Cool and sweaty is exhaustion.

9
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:34.075
Hot and confused is stroke, and stroke is an emergency.

10
00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:38.725
Cool them hard with water and fanning, and call for help immediately.

11
00:00:38.975 --> 00:00:42.875
Hypothermia happens here too, after time in the water.

12
00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:47.875
Take off the wet clothes, insulate the person, and warm the core gently.

13
00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:51.525
Don’t rub the arms and legs, and no alcohol.

14
00:00:51.775 --> 00:00:58.925
And prevention beats all of it: shade, sunscreen, and plenty of water, starting before anyone feels thirsty.
